{\bkmkstart AAAAAABAKO}
{\bkmkend AAAAAABAKO}
{
\pard\plain \s61\li360\sa60\sb30\qj\widctlpar\qj\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
\par
{
{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
Sets the key callback. }}\par
{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
This function sets the key callback of the specified window, which is called when a key is pressed, repeated or released.\par
The key functions deal with physical keys, with layout independent {\b key tokens} named after their values in the standard US keyboard layout. If you want to input text, use the {\b character callback} instead.\par
When a window loses input focus, it will generate synthetic key release events for all pressed keys with associated key tokens. You can tell these events from user-generated events by the fact that the synthetic ones are generated after the focus loss event has been processed, i.e. after the {\b window focus callback} has been called.\par
The scancode of a key is specific to that platform or sometimes even to that machine. Scancodes are intended to allow users to bind keys that don't have a GLFW key token. Such keys have {\f2 key}  set to {\f2 GLFW_KEY_UNKNOWN} , their state is not saved and so it cannot be queried with {\b glfwGetKey}.\par
Sometimes GLFW needs to generate synthetic key events, in which case the scancode may be zero.\par
